// Brickford — lecture summaries, AI 310 (LLM Engineering)
//
// T-033. Every entry here was written FROM its source after reading the whole of
// it: a CS336 lecture from its transcript, a Unit II reading from the page the
// lesson links (fetched from the official repository that the page is built
// from, where the page's own host is unreachable — see the T-033 notes). Nothing
// is written from memory or from what a lecture "probably" covers. A lecture
// whose source could not be fetched has no entry; T-033's unavailable.md says
// which and why.
//
// Same shape as the other summary files, plus two optional fields on each check:
//   at  — the second in the video where the point is taught (video sources);
//   src — the section of the reading where it is taught (papers and readings).
// tools/verify-content.js holds the quiz to its size (5 / 6 / 8 by length, 6 for
// a reading), recomputes every numeric answer independently, and bounds how
// often the answer is the unique longest option, so a check cannot be passed by
// picking the longest one.
//
// No concepts and no figures: DAR.CONCEPTS and DAR.FIG are linear algebra, so
// `concepts: []` is the correct value here.
window.DAR = window.DAR || {};

DAR.SUMMARIES = Object.assign(DAR.SUMMARIES || {}, {

  "ai310.1.0": {
    takeaway: "One call to pipeline() hides three steps — preprocess, run the model, post-process — and choosing a model is mostly choosing which half of the Transformer the task needs: an encoder to understand the whole input, a decoder to generate, or both to turn one sequence into another.",
    beats: [
      { t: "NLP is the field, LLMs are the current tool", d: "NLP covers classifying sentences, labelling each word, extracting answers and generating text. LLMs are a subset defined by scale, by doing many tasks without task-specific training, and by learning from examples in the prompt. Their limits come with them: hallucination, bias, finite context windows and heavy compute." },
      { t: "pipeline() is three steps in one call", d: "The text is preprocessed into something the model can read, the model runs, and its raw predictions are post-processed into a label and a score. The default model for a task is downloaded once and cached; running it again reuses the cached weights, not a cached answer." },
      { t: "The task decides what the output can be", d: "Zero-shot classification takes the candidate labels at call time, so no fine-tuning on your labels is needed. Question answering EXTRACTS a span from the context you pass; it does not generate an answer. Named-entity recognition regroups sub-word pieces such as S, ##yl, ##va, ##in back into one entity." },
      { t: "Pretrain once, fine-tune many times", d: "Language models are pretrained self-supervised — the objective is computed from the text itself, causal (next word) or masked (fill the blank). Fine-tuning then trains on labelled data for one task, needing far less data, time and money than training from scratch, which is why sharing weights matters." },
      { t: "Encoder, decoder, and the mask that keeps training honest", d: "The encoder's attention sees the whole sentence; the decoder generates and may only attend to words already produced. In training the decoder is fed the whole target, so an attention mask hides positions at and after the word being predicted. Architecture is the skeleton; a checkpoint is the weights loaded into it." },
      { t: "Every task is a body plus a head", d: "BERT for classification adds a linear head on the final [CLS] state; for token classification, a head per token; for question answering, start and end logits for a span. GPT-2 predicts the next token with labels shifted by one. ViT cuts an image into 16x16 patches and treats them as tokens, with a [CLS] token just like BERT." },
      { t: "Pick the architecture by asking three questions", d: "Bidirectional or one-way understanding? Generating new text or analysing existing text? Transforming one sequence into another? Classification, NER and extractive QA point to an encoder; open generation and chat to a decoder; translation and summarisation to an encoder-decoder. Full attention is quadratic in length, which is what Longformer's local windows and Reformer's LSH attention work around." },
      { t: "Inference has two phases with different costs", d: "Prefill tokenizes and processes the whole prompt at once, which is computationally intensive; it sets the time to first token. Decode then produces one token at a time, each step attending to everything before it, which is memory-intensive. Temperature, top-k, top-p, repetition penalties and stop sequences shape what decode picks; the KV cache trades memory for not recomputing the past." },
      { t: "Bias survives fine-tuning", d: "bert-base-uncased, trained on Wikipedia and BookCorpus rather than the open web, still fills 'This woman works as a [MASK]' with gendered jobs. Fine-tuning on your own data does not remove what pretraining put there." },
    ],
    worked: "Before reaching for a model, name the task's output. A label for the whole text is a classification head on an encoder; a label per token is token classification; a span copied out of a document is extractive QA; new text is a decoder, or an encoder-decoder when the new text is a transformation of an input. Then find a checkpoint already fine-tuned for that task on the Hub and run it through pipeline() before writing any training code.",
    watch: "Treating the question-answering pipeline as if it could answer from knowledge. It extracts a span from the context it is given, so a context that does not contain the answer still yields a confident-looking span — just a wrong one.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "You call a sentiment-analysis pipeline on a raw sentence and get back a label and a score. What happened in between?", opts: ["The text was preprocessed into model inputs, the model ran, and its predictions were post-processed into a label and score", "The model read the raw string directly; pipeline() only chose which pretrained checkpoint to download for the task", "A pretrained model was briefly fine-tuned on your sentence, and the fine-tuned copy produced the label and score", "The sentence was looked up in a cache of earlier answers, and a stored prediction was returned when one matched"], a: 0, src: "1.3 Transformers, what can they do? — Working with pipelines",
        expl: "pipeline() connects a model with its preprocessing and postprocessing. The model never sees a raw string, and nothing is trained at call time. What gets cached is the downloaded model, so a second run skips the download — not the computation." },
      { q: "The question-answering pipeline is given a question and a context paragraph. What can its answer be?", opts: ["Any sentence the model composes from its training data, guided by the context", "A summary of the context rewritten so that it addresses the question", "Only a span copied out of the context, located by start and end positions", "A yes or no, with the context used only to compute the confidence score"], a: 2, src: "1.3 Question answering; 1.5 Question answering",
        expl: "The pipeline extracts information from the provided context; it does not generate. Under the hood a span-classification head predicts start and end logits over the context's tokens, so the answer is always a piece of the text you passed in." },
      { q: "During training a translation decoder is fed the entire target sentence at once. Why can it not simply copy the word it is asked to predict?", opts: ["Because the target sentence is shuffled before it reaches the decoder", "Because an attention mask hides that position and every later one", "Because the decoder reads only the encoder's output, never the target", "Because training uses a different language for the target each time"], a: 1, src: "1.4 The original architecture",
        expl: "Feeding the whole target speeds training, so the decoder is masked: predicting the fourth word, its attention can use positions 1 to 3 only. The second attention layer still sees the encoder's output for the WHOLE source sentence, because word order differs between languages." },
      { q: "ViT splits a 224 x 224 image into non-overlapping 16 x 16 patches. How many patch embeddings does the encoder receive, before the [CLS] token is added?", num: 196, src: "1.5 Image classification",
        expl: "Each side holds 224 / 16 = 14 patches, so there are 14 x 14 = 196. Each patch becomes one embedding — the image is 'tokenized' into patches — and a learnable [CLS] embedding is prepended, so the sequence is 197 long." },
      { q: "You need to tag every word of a sentence as person, organisation, location or none. Which architecture does the chapter point you to, and why?", opts: ["Decoder-only, because modern LLMs are decoders and handle every task best", "Encoder-decoder, because the output is itself a sequence of labels to generate", "Encoder-only, because each word's label depends on context on both sides of it", "Decoder-only, because labelling one word at a time is a left-to-right process"], a: 2, src: "1.6 Choosing the right architecture",
        expl: "Named-entity recognition is understanding, not generation: a word's label can depend on the words after it as well as before. Encoders attend in both directions, and a token-classification head on top gives one label per token." },
      { q: "Users say the first word of a chatbot's reply takes a long time to appear, but the rest then streams quickly. Which phase is the bottleneck?", opts: ["Decode, because each new token must attend to all previous ones", "Prefill, because the whole prompt is processed at once first", "Sampling, because top-p must sort the vocabulary every step", "Tokenization of the output, which runs after generation ends"], a: 1, src: "1.8 The two-phase inference process; Key performance metrics",
        expl: "Time to first token is primarily set by prefill, which tokenizes and runs the entire input through the model before anything is generated. Decode then governs time per output token; it is the memory-intensive phase, and the KV cache is what speeds it up." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.1.1": {
    takeaway: "Supervised fine-tuning is next-token cross-entropy on your own examples — so the decisions that matter are which tokens carry the loss, what format the data is in, and whether the end-of-sequence token matches the chat template.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The whole method in one call", d: "SFTTrainer takes a model name and a training dataset and trains. Everything else on the page is a choice about the data, the loss mask or the cost of the run." },
      { t: "Four dataset shapes", d: "Language modelling (a text field) or prompt-completion, each either standard (plain strings) or conversational (role and content messages). A conversational dataset has the chat template applied automatically. Anything else is mapped into one of these first — the medical-reasoning example folds Question, chain of thought and Response into a prompt and a completion." },
      { t: "The loss is the pretraining loss, on chosen tokens", d: "Token-level cross-entropy: minimise $-\\sum_t \\log p_\\theta(y_t \\mid y_{\\lt t})$. Prompt and completion are concatenated and tokenized together; the labels are the inputs shifted by one; padding positions get the ignore index (-100) so they add nothing to the loss." },
      { t: "What the logs mean", d: "loss is averaged over NON-masked tokens; mean_token_accuracy is the share of non-masked tokens whose top-1 prediction is right; entropy is the spread of the predicted distribution; grad_norm is measured before clipping; num_tokens counts what has been processed." },
      { t: "Choosing which tokens teach", d: "With a prompt-completion dataset the loss covers the completion only, by default (completion_only_loss=False turns that off). With a conversational dataset, assistant_only_loss=True restricts it to the assistant's replies — which needs generation and endgeneration markers in the chat template; TRL patches them in for known families such as Qwen3." },
      { t: "Making runs cheaper", d: "packing=True fills each sequence with several examples. model_init_kwargs passes loading options such as bfloat16 through SFTConfig. A LoraConfig as peft_config trains adapters instead of the whole model, usually at a higher learning rate (about 1e-4) because only the new parameters are learned." },
      { t: "Instruction tuning a base model", d: "Needs a chat template — role markers, special tokens, turn boundaries — and a conversational dataset; chat_template_path can borrow one from another model. A base model that already ships a template still needs its EOS token aligned with it (eos_token in SFTConfig), or replies do not terminate correctly." },
      { t: "Tools and images", d: "Tool-calling data carries tool calls, tool-role responses and a tools column of JSON schemas. Vision-language data adds image or images columns; set max_length=None there, because truncation can cut image tokens and break training." },
    ],
    worked: "Before the first run, write down which tokens of one example should carry loss, then check the setting that does it: completion-only for prompt-completion data, assistant_only_loss for conversations, nothing extra for plain text. Then train for a few steps and read mean_token_accuracy and loss — both are over non-masked tokens, so they tell you whether the mask is what you meant.",
    watch: "Fine-tuning a base model into a chat model and leaving the EOS token as it was. The model learns the template's turns but never the token that ends them, so it keeps generating past its answer; the fix is eos_token in SFTConfig, matched to the template.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "What does SFTTrainer minimise for each training example?", opts: ["A reward that a separate preference model assigns to the whole completion", "The squared distance between output and reference embeddings", "The number of positions where the output differs from the reference", "The negative log-likelihood of each target token given those before it"], a: 3, src: "Looking deeper into the SFT method — Computing the loss",
        expl: "SFT is token-level cross-entropy, the same objective as pretraining: $-\\sum_t \\log p_\\theta(y_t \\mid y_{\\lt t})$. The labels are the input shifted by one position, and masked positions (padding, or tokens excluded by the loss settings) contribute nothing. No reward model is involved; that is the next stage of alignment." },
      { q: "You pass a prompt-completion dataset to SFTTrainer and change no settings. Which tokens does the loss see?", opts: ["Only the completion tokens; the prompt tokens are masked out", "Every token, prompt and completion alike, just as in pretraining", "Only the prompt tokens, so the model learns to read inputs", "Only the last token of each completion, just before EOS"], a: 0, src: "Customization — Train on completion only",
        expl: "For prompt-completion data the trainer computes the loss on the completion only by default; completion_only_loss=False puts the prompt back in. The prompt is still in the input — the model conditions on it — it just is not something the model is graded on reproducing." },
      { q: "Default completion-only loss. A logging interval holds one example: 4 prompt tokens, all predicted correctly at top-1, and 6 completion tokens, of which 3 are predicted correctly. What mean_token_accuracy is logged?", num: 0.5, src: "Logged metrics; Train on completion only",
        expl: "mean_token_accuracy is the proportion of NON-masked tokens whose top-1 prediction matches. The prompt is masked, so it is in neither the numerator nor the denominator: 3 / 6 = 0.5. Counting it would give 7 / 10 = 0.7 and flatter the model with tokens it was never trained to produce." },
      { q: "assistant_only_loss=True needs the chat template to contain generation and endgeneration markers. What job are those markers doing?", opts: ["Telling the tokenizer where each turn ends so that turns can be packed", "Switching the model into generation mode, which training turns off", "Placing the EOS token after every user message in the conversation", "Marking which rendered tokens are the reply, the only ones given loss"], a: 3, src: "Customization — Train on assistant messages only",
        expl: "Once a conversation is rendered through the template it is one token sequence; the markers are how the trainer knows which stretch of it is the assistant's response, so that the user and system messages can be left out of the loss. For known families such as Qwen3, TRL patches the markers in itself." },
      { q: "When SFTTrainer trains LoRA adapters rather than the whole model, the page suggests a higher learning rate, about 1e-4. Why?", opts: ["Because the frozen base weights need a stronger push to move", "Because only the new adapter parameters are being learned", "Because adapters are trained in lower precision than the model", "Because packing is turned on whenever PEFT is in use"], a: 1, src: "Customization — Train adapters with PEFT",
        expl: "With an adapter the pretrained weights stay frozen and only the small set of new parameters is trained from scratch, which tolerates — and needs — larger steps than nudging an already-trained network. The frozen weights do not move at any learning rate." },
      { q: "You instruction-tune a Qwen base model whose tokenizer already has a chat template, and the result never stops generating. What does the page say must be aligned?", opts: ["The learning rate, which must be lower for chat-tuned models", "The packing flag, which merges turns and hides their ends", "The EOS token, which must match the template's end-of-turn token", "The max_length, which must be None so that no turn is ever truncated"], a: 2, src: "Instruction tuning example",
        expl: "A base model with a predefined template does not need clone_chat_template, but its EOS token must be aligned with the template — eos_token in SFTConfig, for example the template's end-of-turn token — so that the model learns to emit the token that ends a reply." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.2": {
    takeaway: "Quantization buys memory with precision: the weights are stored in fewer bits and accuracy is preserved as far as the method can manage — and which method fits is decided by your hardware and by what you need to do next (load, fine-tune, compile, save), which is what the compatibility table lays out.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What is being traded", d: "Weights are normally stored in full precision (fp32); half precision (fp16, bf16) is increasingly the norm for large models; some methods go on to integers such as int8 or int4. Fewer bits per weight means less memory to load and run the model, at some cost in accuracy that each method tries to minimise." },
      { t: "Calibrated or on the fly", d: "Some methods need a calibration step to reach greater accuracy and extreme compression (1-2 bits). Others quantize on the fly: point them at a full-precision checkpoint and they work out of the box." },
      { t: "Choose by need, not by name", d: "The quantization picker asks for a model, your hardware, and what the method must do: load on the fly, fine-tune with PEFT, compile with torch.compile(), or save the result. It shows whether the model fits at each precision — counting the KV cache, not just the weights — and lists the methods that match." },
      { t: "The table's columns are the tradeoffs", d: "For each method: on-the-fly or not; CPU, CUDA, ROCm, Metal and Intel GPU support; torch.compile; the bit widths; PEFT fine-tuning; whether Transformers can serialize the result. A method is a row of answers to those questions." },
      { t: "bitsandbytes: load and fine-tune in one step", d: "On the fly, 4 or 8 bits, CUDA and CPU and Intel GPU (partial on ROCm and Metal), PEFT fine-tuning supported, serializable. The fit when you want to take a downloaded model straight to a quantized fine-tune." },
      { t: "AWQ: 4-bit, prepared ahead", d: "Not on the fly — the model is quantized beforehand and loaded already quantized. 4 bits, CUDA, ROCm, CPU and Intel GPU, no Metal; PEFT and serialization supported." },
      { t: "GGUF: the llama.cpp format", d: "1 to 8 bits, runs on CPU, CUDA, Metal and Intel GPU, compiles — but it is not on the fly, does not support PEFT fine-tuning, and is not serializable with Transformers. It is for running a model, not for training it further." },
      { t: "Tools for doing it without code", d: "Community Spaces convert a repository for you (bitsandbytes, GGUF and MLX), and an AutoQuant notebook runs several methods; DeepLearning.AI's two short courses are the suggested start for newcomers." },
    ],
    worked: "Fix the constraints in order and let them eliminate rows. Hardware first (a Mac rules out AWQ; Metal favours GGUF), then the next step (fine-tuning with PEFT rules out GGUF; loading straight from a full-precision checkpoint needs an on-the-fly method such as bitsandbytes), then whether the result must be saved with Transformers. Whatever survives is the shortlist; only then compare bit widths.",
    watch: "Sizing the GPU from the quantized weights alone. The picker counts the KV cache as well, and at long context that can be what no longer fits even after the weights have been cut to a quarter.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "According to the overview, what does quantization trade, and for what?", opts: ["Precision of the stored weights, for lower memory, while keeping as much accuracy as it can", "Context length, for a smaller model, while keeping the same weights at the same precision", "Training speed, for faster inference, by retraining the model in a lower-precision format", "Nothing at all: it is a lossless re-encoding of exactly the same weights into a smaller file"], a: 0, src: "Overview",
        expl: "Quantization stores the weights in a lower precision — fp32 to fp16 or bf16, or further to int8 or int4 — which lowers the memory needed to load and use the model. It is lossy: each method tries to preserve as much accuracy as possible, which is why some need calibration." },
      { q: "A model's weights take 16 GB stored in bf16. Ignoring the small overhead of scales and metadata, how many GB do the same weights take at int4?", num: 4, src: "Overview",
        expl: "bf16 is 16 bits per weight and int4 is 4, so each weight shrinks by a factor of 4: 16 GB / 4 = 4 GB. From fp32 (32 bits) the same model would have been 32 GB, which is why half precision is already the norm for large models." },
      { q: "You need weights at 2 bits per parameter. What does the overview tell you to expect?", opts: ["Any on-the-fly method will do, since bit width is only a loading flag", "Nothing different from 4-bit: every method supports every bit width", "2 bits is below what quantization can do; you would need distillation", "A method that needs a calibration step, for accuracy at that compression"], a: 3, src: "Overview",
        expl: "The overview splits methods into those needing calibration — 'for greater accuracy and extreme compression (1-2 bits)' — and those that work out of the box on the fly. Bit width is a property of the method, too: the table's bits column differs row by row." },
      { q: "You want to load a freshly downloaded full-precision model in 4-bit on an NVIDIA GPU and fine-tune it with LoRA, with no separate quantization pass. Of these three, which does the table support?", opts: ["bitsandbytes, which quantizes on the fly and supports PEFT", "AWQ, since it is 4-bit and it supports PEFT fine-tuning too", "GGUF, since llama.cpp runs on CUDA as well as on a CPU", "None of them; all three need the model quantized first"], a: 0, src: "Full compatibility table",
        expl: "Two columns decide it. On the fly: bitsandbytes yes, AWQ and GGUF no — they load a model that was quantized beforehand. PEFT fine-tuning: bitsandbytes and AWQ yes, GGUF no. Only bitsandbytes passes both." },
      { q: "You want to run a quantized model locally on an Apple Silicon Mac. Of GGUF, AWQ and bitsandbytes, which does the table mark as fully supported on Metal?", opts: ["AWQ, because 4-bit kernels exist for every GPU backend", "GGUF, the llama.cpp format, which is supported on Metal", "bitsandbytes, because quantizing on the fly avoids kernels", "All three, because int4 arithmetic is the same on any chip"], a: 1, src: "Full compatibility table",
        expl: "Support is per backend, not per bit width. GGUF is green on Metal; AWQ is red there; bitsandbytes is only partial (yellow). Quantizing on the fly still needs kernels for the hardware it runs on." },
      { q: "The picker shows a model's int4 weights fitting on your GPU, yet at long context the run still runs out of memory. What did the picker count that the weights alone do not?", opts: ["The optimizer states, which inference also has to hold", "The calibration data, which stays loaded after quantizing", "The KV cache, which grows with the length of the context", "The compiled graph cache that torch.compile() keeps"], a: 2, src: "Overview — the quantization picker",
        expl: "The picker shows whether the model fits at each precision 'including the KV cache'. Quantizing shrinks the weights, but the keys and values cached for every token of the context still need memory, and that grows with context length." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.3": {
    takeaway: "The harness turns 'how good is this model?' into a reproducible command — but only if you know what the number depends on: the backend and its parallel layout, whether the model exposes logprobs, how answers were extracted, and whose prompt procedure the task follows.",
    beats: [
      { t: "One framework, many benchmarks", d: "Over 60 standard academic benchmarks with hundreds of subtasks, run against models from transformers, vLLM, commercial APIs and more. It is the backend of Hugging Face's Open LLM Leaderboard, and it uses publicly available prompts so that results are reproducible and comparable between papers." },
      { t: "Install the core, then a backend", d: "The base package no longer pulls in transformers or torch; each backend is an extra — lm_eval[hf], lm_eval[vllm], lm_eval[api]. lm-eval ls tasks lists what can be run." },
      { t: "The hf backend passes model_args straight through", d: "Anything AutoModel accepts works: a local path, a Hub revision to evaluate a partially trained checkpoint, a dtype, a PEFT adapter (peft=PATH), delta weights, a GPTQ model. batch_size auto finds the largest batch that fits, and auto:N recomputes it N times for tasks whose examples vary a lot in length." },
      { t: "GGUF needs its tokenizer", d: "The hf backend can evaluate a GGUF file, but without a separate tokenizer it rebuilds one from the GGUF — which can take hours or hang. The gguf model type instead scores a llama.cpp server, computing loglikelihoods by exact teacher forcing; perplexity (loglikelihood_rolling) is not implemented there." },
      { t: "Two kinds of multi-GPU", d: "If the model fits on one GPU, accelerate launch gives every GPU its own full copy: K GPUs, K times faster. If it does not fit, parallelize=True splits the weights across GPUs. Both can be combined. Native tensor parallelism (tp_plan=auto) needs the number of key-value heads divisible by the TP degree. Multi-node is not supported natively." },
      { t: "vLLM and SGLang for speed, HF as the reference", d: "vLLM runs tensor parallel and data parallel together; each data-parallel replica is a ray actor holding tensor_parallel_size GPUs. Its outputs occasionally differ from Hugging Face's, which the harness treats as the reference — a comparator script checks one against the other." },
      { t: "No logprobs, no likelihood tasks", d: "A model that returns no logits or logprobs — most chat APIs — can run generate_until tasks only. loglikelihood, loglikelihood_rolling and multiple_choice need the probabilities of the prompt's continuations. For generative tasks on closed chat models, read a few outputs first (--limit 10) to confirm answer extraction and scoring work." },
      { t: "Keep the evidence", d: "--output_path saves results and --log_samples saves every response for post-hoc analysis; --use_cache resumes interrupted runs (per GPU count); --check_integrity verifies task data; --predict_only returns generations without scoring, for tasks that would execute untrusted code; write_out.py shows exactly what the model is fed." },
      { t: "Whose procedure a task follows", d: "In order: what people who train LLMs agree on, an official implementation, what evaluators agree on, then the harness's own pick among common ones. The point is to reduce the harm when people compare runs across papers — which the project discourages, and which happens anyway." },
    ],
    worked: "Treat an evaluation as an experiment with a config. Fix the backend, the model_args (revision, dtype, adapters), the parallel layout and the task version; run with --log_samples and --output_path; read some logged samples before reading the score; and when two backends disagree, check against the hf backend with the comparator before believing either.",
    watch: "Reading a generative-task score from a chat API without looking at its outputs. If the answer extraction does not match the model's response format, correct answers are scored as wrong, and the number measures the parser rather than the model — which is why the README asks for --limit 10 first.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Evaluating a GGUF model through the hf backend seems to hang before the first task starts. What does the README identify as the cause and the fix?", opts: ["The GGUF file must be converted to safetensors first; convert it, then rerun", "The hf backend cannot read GGUF at all; serve the model with llama.cpp instead", "No separate tokenizer was passed, so one is being rebuilt from the GGUF file", "The batch size is too large for a quantized model; set batch_size to auto"], a: 2, src: "Basic Usage — Evaluating GGUF Models",
        expl: "Without a tokenizer path, Hugging Face reconstructs the tokenizer from the GGUF file, which can take hours or hang indefinitely. Passing tokenizer=/path/to/tokenizer in model_args cuts that from hours to seconds. The hf backend does read GGUF — via pretrained= the folder and gguf_file= the file." },
      { q: "Your model fits comfortably on one GPU and you have four. Which setup does the README use to make the evaluation about four times faster?", opts: ["parallelize=True, which splits the model's weights across all four GPUs", "tp_plan=auto, which shards the weights with native tensor parallelism", "batch_size auto:4, which recomputes the largest batch four times", "accelerate launch, so that each GPU loads its own full copy of the model"], a: 3, src: "Multi-GPU Evaluation with Hugging Face accelerate",
        expl: "Data parallelism: each GPU holds a separate full copy and takes a share of the examples, so K GPUs are K times faster. parallelize=True and tp_plan split ONE copy across GPUs — the tool for a model too large for one GPU, not a speed-up for one that fits." },
      { q: "With the vllm backend you pass tensor_parallel_size=2 and data_parallel_size=4. How many GPUs does the run reserve?", num: 8, src: "Tensor + Data Parallel and Optimized Inference with vLLM",
        expl: "Each of the 4 data-parallel replicas is dispatched as a separate ray actor, and each actor reserves tensor_parallel_size = 2 GPUs because its copy of the model is split across two. 4 x 2 = 8 GPUs: tensor parallelism splits a model, data parallelism multiplies it." },
      { q: "You want to score a chat-completions API model on a multiple-choice task that is scored by loglikelihood, and the API returns no logprobs. What does the README say?", opts: ["It runs, but the harness has to estimate the logprobs by sampling", "It cannot: without logprobs, only generate_until tasks can be run", "It runs once you pass the API model's own tokenizer to the harness", "It cannot, because API models only run perplexity-style tasks"], a: 1, src: "Model APIs and Inference Servers",
        expl: "Likelihood tasks rank answer options by the probability the model assigns to each continuation, so they need logits or logprobs. Models that supply none — the ChatCompletions and Anthropic rows say 'no logprobs' — can run generate_until tasks only." },
      { q: "Before trusting a generative-task score from a closed chat model, what does the README recommend doing?", opts: ["Run the whole task twice and average, as chat APIs sample at random", "Raise max_retries, because failed calls are scored as wrong answers", "Switch to the completions endpoint, the reference for every score", "Read a few outputs with --limit 10 to confirm answers are extracted"], a: 3, src: "Model APIs and Inference Servers (note on closed chat models)",
        expl: "A generative task is scored by extracting an answer from free text. If the extraction does not fit the model's response format, the score measures the parser, not the model — so look at about ten outputs first, and if needed use a system prompt in model_args to set the format." },
      { q: "When implementing a task, the harness prefers the procedure LLM trainers agree on, then an official implementation, before its own choice. What is that order for?", opts: ["So its numbers agree with other groups' runs, as people compare across papers anyway", "So every task scores a little higher, which makes the leaderboard easier to read", "So that no prompt is ever made public, which keeps tasks out of training data", "So the harness can skip its own validation, since agreed procedures are known correct"], a: 0, src: "Contributing — Implementing new tasks",
        expl: "The stated aim is to decrease the harm when people inevitably compare runs across different papers, a practice the project discourages. Its prompts are deliberately public — that is what makes results reproducible — and the order is a guideline that can be overruled." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.4": {
    takeaway: "vLLM's speed comes from treating the KV cache as managed memory — paged into fixed-size blocks, shared between requests by hashing whole prefixes, evicted least-recently-used — and its usefulness from serving all of that behind an OpenAI-compatible API.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What vLLM is", d: "A library for LLM inference and serving, begun at Berkeley's Sky Computing Lab and now a large open-source project. Three ways in: run models (the Quickstart), build applications (the User Guide), or build vLLM itself (the Developer Guide)." },
      { t: "Why it is fast", d: "State-of-the-art serving throughput; efficient management of attention key and value memory with PagedAttention; continuous batching, chunked prefill and prefix caching; CUDA graphs; quantization from FP8 to INT4, GPTQ, AWQ and GGUF; optimized attention kernels; speculative decoding; and prefill, decode and encode that can be disaggregated." },
      { t: "Why it is flexible", d: "Hugging Face models (200+ architectures, from decoder-only to MoE, state-space and multimodal), parallel sampling and beam search, tensor, pipeline, data, expert and context parallelism, streaming, structured outputs, tool calling, multi-LoRA, an OpenAI-compatible server, and a wide range of hardware." },
      { t: "Offline batched inference", d: "LLM loads the model and engine; SamplingParams sets temperature, top-p and the rest; llm.generate adds the prompts to the engine's waiting queue and runs it for throughput. Unless told otherwise, vLLM applies the model repository's generation_config.json; generation_config='vllm' restores vLLM's own defaults." },
      { t: "generate is not chat", d: "llm.generate does not apply the model's chat template. For an Instruct or Chat model, apply the template yourself with the tokenizer, or call llm.chat with OpenAI-style message lists." },
      { t: "Online serving", d: "vllm serve MODEL starts a server on localhost:8000 implementing the OpenAI API — list models, completions, chat completions — so existing OpenAI clients work by changing the base URL. One model at a time; --api-key makes it check keys (several, for rotation). The attention backend is chosen automatically, or set with --attention-backend." },
      { t: "Prefix caching: reuse, do not recompute", d: "The KV-cache blocks of processed requests are kept and reused when a new request starts with the same prefix — almost a free lunch, since outputs do not change. Each block's hash combines its parent block's hash, its own tokens, and extras such as LoRA IDs, image hashes or a cache salt; only full blocks are cached." },
      { t: "A block pool with an LRU free queue", d: "All blocks are allocated up front as a pool, each with a reference count. A request reusing cached blocks 'touches' them out of the free queue; new blocks are popped from its head, evicting whatever cached block was least recently used. A finished request's blocks are freed in reverse order, so its last block — hashing the longest prefix, least likely to be reused — goes first." },
      { t: "Isolation for shared deployments", d: "A per-request cache_salt is folded into the first block's hash, so only requests with the same salt can share cached blocks. That blocks timing attacks in which one tenant infers another's cached content from latency." },
    ],
    worked: "To serve a model: vllm serve it, then point the openai client's base_url at http://localhost:8000/v1 and use completions or chat completions unchanged. To get prefix-cache hits, put the shared part of every request — system prompt, long document — at the very start, identical token for token, because reuse is decided block by block from the first token.",
    watch: "Expecting prefix caching to reuse any shared text. A block is reusable only if it is full and every token before it matches too, so a shared passage after a differing first line, or a partial final block, is recomputed.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "The landing page lists PagedAttention first among what makes vLLM fast. What does it manage efficiently?", opts: ["The model weights, paging each layer in from disk as it is needed", "The memory that holds the attention keys and values for requests", "The queue of incoming requests, batching them as they arrive", "The precision of the attention scores, storing them as FP8"], a: 1, src: "Welcome to vLLM — vLLM is fast with",
        expl: "PagedAttention is 'efficient management of attention key and value memory' — the KV cache, held in blocks rather than one contiguous slab per request. Batching requests as they arrive is a separate feature (continuous batching), and so is quantization." },
      { q: "You load an Instruct model with LLM and call llm.generate on plain prompt strings, and the answers look wrong. What does the quickstart point out?", opts: ["generate needs SamplingParams, or else it decodes greedily from the prompt", "Instruct models can be used only through the OpenAI-compatible server", "generate does not apply the chat template: apply it, or use llm.chat", "The prompts must be tokenized first, since generate expects token IDs"], a: 2, src: "Quickstart — Offline Batched Inference",
        expl: "An Instruct model was trained on text in its chat template, and llm.generate passes your string through as-is. Apply the template with the tokenizer (apply_chat_template with add_generation_prompt) or call llm.chat with message lists. The server, by contrast, uses the tokenizer's template by default." },
      { q: "You create LLM without passing SamplingParams, and the sampling defaults turn out not to be vLLM's own. Why?", opts: ["vLLM applies the model repository's generation_config.json by default", "vLLM reuses the defaults of the last server started on that machine", "vLLM picks sampling defaults at random unless a seed is supplied", "vLLM reads its defaults from the OpenAI API that it is imitating"], a: 0, src: "Quickstart — Offline Batched Inference; Online Serving",
        expl: "If the Hugging Face repository has a generation_config.json, vLLM uses the sampling values the model's creator recommends. To use vLLM's defaults instead, pass generation_config='vllm' to LLM, or --generation-config vllm to vllm serve." },
      { q: "Two requests contain an identical 4-token block, but the tokens before that block differ. Can the second request reuse the first one's cached copy of the block?", opts: ["Yes, because a block's hash is computed from its own four tokens only", "Yes, but only if both requests use the same sampling parameters", "No, because cached blocks are never shared between two requests", "No, because each block's hash folds in its parent's, so its prefix"], a: 3, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching",
        expl: "vLLM hashes each block by its own tokens AND the hash of the block before it, so a block is identified by everything up to its end. That is exactly right for a KV cache: a token's keys and values depend on all the tokens before it, so the same four tokens after a different prefix have different K and V." },
      { q: "Block size 16. An earlier request with a 64-token prompt is fully cached. A new 50-token prompt matches it in its first 40 tokens and then differs. How many of the new prompt's tokens are served from the prefix cache?", num: 32, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching — Example",
        expl: "Only full blocks that match token for token, from the start, are hits. Tokens 1-16 and 17-32 match; the third block (33-48) matches only 8 of its 16 tokens, so it misses — and every block after a miss has a different parent hash. 2 x 16 = 32 tokens hit; 18 are computed." },
      { q: "When a request finishes, vLLM adds its freed blocks to the free queue in reverse order, so its last block is evicted first. Why that order?", opts: ["The last block was the one written most recently, so it is the coldest", "Its last block hashes the longest prefix, so it is least likely reused", "The last block is usually partly full, so it can never be cached", "Reversing the order makes queue moves O(1) instead of O(n)"], a: 1, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching — Free",
        expl: "A later block is identified by a longer prefix, so fewer future requests will match it; the first blocks — a shared system prompt, say — are the likeliest hits. Evicting from the head of the free queue then throws away the least useful blocks first. The O(1) moves come from the doubly linked list, not from the order." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.5": {
    takeaway: "An LLM can only emit text, so 'tool use' is a protocol: the system prompt describes the tools, the model writes an action in an agreed format and stops, the agent parses and runs it, and the result goes back into the prompt as an observation — Thought, Action, Observation, in a loop until the task is done.",
    beats: [
      { t: "An agent is a model with a body", d: "A system that uses an AI model to interact with its environment to reach a user's objective. The brain — usually an LLM — reasons, plans and decides which action to take; the body is the set of tools it has been given, and it can do nothing those tools do not allow." },
      { t: "Agency is a spectrum", d: "Simple processor (output changes nothing), router (output picks a branch), tool caller (output picks the function and its arguments), multi-step agent (output decides whether the loop continues), multi-agent (one agentic workflow starts another)." },
      { t: "The brain predicts tokens until EOS", d: "LLMs are mostly decoders predicting the next token from the previous ones, autoregressively, until they emit the end-of-sequence token. Each model has its own special tokens to open and close messages and turns." },
      { t: "Messages are a UI abstraction", d: "Before the model sees a conversation, every message is concatenated into one prompt by the model's chat template; the model does not remember, it rereads everything each turn. The system message sets behaviour and, for an agent, lists the tools, the action format and how to lay out thoughts." },
      { t: "A tool is a function the LLM is told about", d: "It needs a description of what it does, a callable, typed arguments and, optionally, typed outputs. Good tools complement the model — a calculator for arithmetic, a search for anything after its training data. A @tool decorator can build the description from the function's name, docstring and type hints; MCP standardises the interface across frameworks." },
      { t: "Thought, Action, Observation", d: "The LLM decides the next step, the agent calls a tool with arguments, and the result is appended to the prompt for the model to reflect on — a while loop that runs until the objective is met. Chain-of-Thought reasons step by step without tools; ReAct interleaves reasoning with actions and observations; models such as DeepSeek-R1 are trained to think in delimited reasoning tokens." },
      { t: "Actions: JSON, code, or function calls", d: "A JSON agent writes the action as a JSON object; a code agent writes an executable code block, more expressive (loops, conditionals, libraries) but a security risk to run; a function-calling agent is a JSON agent fine-tuned to emit a new message per action. All rely on stop and parse: the model stops once the action is complete, and a parser extracts the tool and its arguments." },
      { t: "The failure the dummy agent shows", d: "Left to generate freely, the model writes its Action and then an Observation of its own — invented weather, with no tool run. The fix is to stop generation at 'Observation:', run the real function, append its output as the Observation, and resume." },
      { t: "Then a real framework", d: "smolagents' CodeAgent does the same loop with code actions: tools defined with type hints and docstrings that describe every argument, a final_answer tool, and a cap on steps (max_steps)." },
    ],
    worked: "Build the loop by hand once. Write a system prompt that lists one tool and the exact action format, call the model with stop set to the observation marker, parse the JSON it produced, run the function, append 'Observation:' and the real result to the assistant message, and call the model again. Every agent framework is this, plus error handling.",
    watch: "Trusting text after the action. A model that is not stopped will happily write the observation itself — fluent, plausible and fabricated — and then a final answer built on it. Stop at the observation marker, and treat only what the tool returned as the source of truth.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "An LLM's output chooses which function to run and with what arguments, but your program runs it once and stops. Where does that sit on the unit's spectrum of agency?", opts: ["Router: the output chooses between two fixed paths in the program", "Simple processor: the output has no effect on what the program does", "Tool caller: the output decides which function runs, and with what", "Multi-step agent: the output controls whether the loop carries on"], a: 2, src: "What is an Agent? — The spectrum of agency",
        expl: "Choosing the function and its arguments is the tool-caller level (run_function(llm_chosen_tool, llm_chosen_args)). It becomes a multi-step agent only when the model's output also decides whether to keep iterating — the while loop of Thought, Action, Observation." },
      { q: "Three turns into a chat with an agent, how does the LLM 'remember' what the user said in the first turn?", opts: ["Every message is concatenated into one prompt, which it rereads in full", "It stores earlier turns in its weights, updating them after every reply", "The system message keeps a summary that the model rewrites every turn", "It does not: each turn is answered from the newest user message only"], a: 0, src: "Messages and Special Tokens",
        expl: "Chat is a UI abstraction. The chat template concatenates all messages, with the model's special tokens marking each turn, into a single prompt; the model reads it in full every time. That is also how tool results get in: they are appended as messages and the whole conversation is sent again." },
      { q: "When an agent 'uses' a weather tool, what does the LLM itself actually do?", opts: ["It calls the weather API itself, over a connection the framework opens", "It writes text describing the call; the agent parses it and runs the tool", "It recalls the weather from its training data, formatted as a tool result", "It hands the conversation to the tool, which then writes the final reply"], a: 1, src: "What are Tools? — How do tools work?",
        expl: "LLMs take text in and give text out; they cannot call anything. Providing a tool means describing it in the system prompt so the model can write a text invocation such as call weather_tool('Paris'). The agent executes it, appends the result, and sends the updated conversation back to the model." },
      { q: "A question needs today's exchange rate. Why does the unit's comparison point to ReAct rather than Chain-of-Thought for it?", opts: ["ReAct reasons in more steps, and more steps make the arithmetic reliable", "Chain-of-Thought is a training technique, so it needs a fine-tuned model", "ReAct interleaves actions and observations; CoT reasons without tools", "Chain-of-Thought cannot be used with chat templates or system messages"], a: 2, src: "Thought: Internal Reasoning and the ReAct Approach",
        expl: "Both reason step by step, but Chain-of-Thought stays inside the model — good for logic and maths — while ReAct alternates thoughts with tool actions and their observations, which is what info-seeking tasks need. Both are prompting strategies; training a model to think in delimited reasoning tokens is the training-level technique." },
      { q: "A code agent writes a Python block as its action instead of a JSON object. What does the unit give as the gain and the cost?", opts: ["Gain: loops, conditionals and libraries; cost: running generated code is risky", "Gain: no parser is needed at all; cost: the model must be fine-tuned for code", "Gain: actions run faster than JSON; cost: it can no longer call outside APIs", "Gain: no system prompt is needed; cost: stop-and-parse no longer applies"], a: 0, src: "Actions — Code Agents",
        expl: "Code can express loops, conditionals, nested functions and library calls that a JSON blob cannot, and is easier to debug. But executing LLM-generated code invites everything from prompt injection to harmful code, so the unit recommends a framework with safeguards. Code agents still stop and parse: the block is delimited and its end is signalled." },
      { q: "In the dummy agent, the model writes an Action and then, by itself, an 'Observation' with plausible weather — though no tool ran. What fix does the unit apply?", opts: ["Lower the temperature, so the model stops inventing its observations", "Put the weather in the system prompt, so no tool call is ever needed", "Ask the model to mark invented text, and filter that out afterwards", "Stop at 'Observation:', then run the tool and insert its real output"], a: 3, src: "Dummy Agent Library",
        expl: "The model was hallucinating — generating an observation rather than receiving one. Passing stop=['Observation:'] ends generation right after the action; the agent then runs get_weather, appends 'Observation:' and the real result to the assistant message, and resumes generation from there." },
    ],
  },

});
